{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are generally below realized prices by ~1–3 €/t across most of the horizon (especially days ~3–20). Apply an upward level shift (+~1.5 to +2.5 €/t) when the recent 10–20d trend is up/sideways.",
    "Underreaction to up-moves: after an upswing in history, the forecast path rises too slowly (lag). Increase the first 7–15 days’ slope/step-up when the last 5–10 days show positive momentum.",
    "Late-horizon crash/overshoot down: at longer horizons (~days 20–30) the model often predicts sharp declines that do not materialize (Example 5). Damp negative moves beyond ~day 15 (e.g., cap cumulative drawdown or blend toward a flat/mean path).",
    "Over-smoothing of peaks: local highs in outcomes (near ~69–70) are systematically underpredicted. Add a convexity/peak premium when recent volatility is elevated and price is breaking above recent range.",
    "Mean reversion miscalibrated: the model reverts too strongly downward after rallies. Reduce mean-reversion strength when the last 20–30d range is rising (higher highs/higher lows)."
  ]
}
